## Preliminary note
This skill tries to make the handling of saved playlists for the player MPD comfortable. There is a very good [MPD skill from forslund](https://github.com/forslund/mpd_skill). If you don't use playlists, but only have a collection of music files, you should use his skill.
If you can answer three or more questions with yes, you should try this skill:
Are different playlists used in MPD?
Is there more than one MPD player in the network?
Is there more than one OVOS system in the house / apartment or are you using Hivemind-Listener Hive-Voice-Satellite?
Do you want to be able to control all MPD players with all OVOS systems?

Under not fully reproducible conditions, conflicts with OVOS-OCP skill/plugin may occur. Please report on this at Issues.

<p>For users who have compiled their own playlists, especially if they include internet radio streams, my skill might be helpful.</p>

## Hints for the configuration of the skill:
Location: /home/$USER/.config/mycroft/skills/ovos-skill-my-mpd-player.joergz2/settings.json
**Be carefull to edit this configuration file. Syntax faults will be punished by deleting the whole file. Use a JSON file checker or an IDE as VSC to generate a valid file.**
After installation you have this settings.json:
`    {
        "__mycroft_skill_firstrun": false,
        "radios": {
            "server1": {
                "ip": "IP_1",
                "port": 6600
            },
            "server2": {
                "ip": "IP_2",
                "port": 6600
            }
        },
        "stations": {
            "station_1": 0,
            "station_2": 1
        }
    }
`
## Notes
General: use lower chars only
Section "radios"
server1: replace with the real name. Name normally refers to a room which MPD device is located. Using Hive-Voice-Satellite name of satellite must be identically to server1 if both are in the same room. This would make the explicit naming of the room in which you are already located superfluous<br>
IP_1: IP address of first MPD server
6600: standard port of MPD. Only cange if another port is set.
You can add as much servers as you want. Last server has no comma at the end (strict JSON syntax needed).

Section "stations"
This is only helpful if the playlist is always the same and the individual items in the playlist are to be selected using a name (station name). You can select a song or a station by calling the position number everytime.

Another difference (from forslund's skill) is that this skill is designed to manage multiple MPD servers. It is rather not well suited to manage an MPD installation on the same device that OVOS is running on. This is because if MPD is running on the same device that OVOS is installed on.

## Conflicts

There is an occasional conflict with the volume-speaker skill. It uses the same words as this skill for volume control. However, in the MPD skill the volume of the output signal of the MPD server is controlled with values from 0 to 100. The volume control in the volume-skill addresses the master channel of the ALSA mixer (in steps from 1-10). You can also think of it like this: MPD has its own preamp that can be controlled and ALSA is the power amp.

If the standard volume-skill is active, this can easily lead to a request for volume increase being countered by the system with the announcement that the maximum has already been reached. Nevertheless, the sound reproduction is too quiet. The reason for this can be a too low "preamplification" of the input channel of ALSA (e.g. "Headphone") or a too low output level of MPD itself. The whole audio topic is still complicated under Linux. There would be other solutions. But this is rather a topic that should be negotiated under issues.

If you don't want to uninstall the default volume-skill, it's best to contact me via Issues to implement another solution.

